    "evidence_text": "Induction of myelodysplasia by myeloid-derived suppressor cells.\n\nBone progenitor dysfunction induces myelodysplasia and secondary leukemia\n\nDeletion of Sbds in mouse osteoprogenitors induced bone marrow dysfunction with myelodysplasia.\n\nHere, we show that the observed spontaneous chromosome breaks are partially suppressed by reducing the cellular oxygen tension.\n\nThe effect of SOD1 can also be modulated by cellular oxygen tension.\n\nPeriosteum contains skeletal stem cells with high bone regenerative potential controlled by Periostin\n\nBone regeneration relies on the activation of skeletal stem cells (SSCs) that still remain poorly characterized.\n\nPeriostin gene deletion impairs PC functions and fracture consolidation.\n\nThere is a concomitant increase in reactive oxygen species (ROS) in these transgenic mice with disease progression.\n\nDNA damage and error-prone repair can be decreased or reversed in vivo by N-acetyl cysteine antioxidant treatment.\n\nThese data suggest treatment strategies that target RAS/RAC pathways and ROS production in human MDS/AML.\n\nPerturbed ribosome biogenesis in DBA has been shown to induce a p53-mediated ribosomal stress response.",
